  "response": "**Most likely cause:** the loyalty and upgrade logic in `src/billing.ts` reads the wall clock and compares the result against day thresholds. Two copies of the same line do this:\n\n```ts\n// src/billing.ts:92 (generateInvoice) and :273 (recommendUpgrade)\nconst daysSince = Math.floor((Date.now() - user.createdAt) / (1000 * 60 * 60 * 24));\nif (daysSince > 365) ... else if (daysSince > 180) ... else if (daysSince > 90) ...   // discounts\nif (daysSince > 7)  ... if (daysSince > 30) ... if (daysSince > 14) ...                // upgrade copy\n```\n\nThis flakes at day boundaries for three reasons:\n1. **It reads the real clock.** A fixture like \"user created 365 days ago\" gives a different `daysSince` depending on when the test runs relative to when `createdAt` was set.\n2. **It counts elapsed time, not calendar days.** `floor(ms / 86_400_000)` with a strict `>` means a user created on a calendar date crosses a threshold at whatever time of day they were created, not at midnight.\n3. **It ignores DST.** A span that includes a spring-forward is an hour short, so `floor` can drop a whole day. The same test can pass in one season or timezone (local vs. CI in UTC) and fail in another.\n\nA wrong `daysSince` changes `discountCents`, so the invoice total is off by 2%, 5% or 10%.\n\n## How this lines up with the scan\n\n- **crime_00029** (direct clock reads in `billing.ts`, lines 57, 63, 92, 198, 238, 250, 273) is the relevant finding. But it's mislabelled: it says \"none decide a branch — all 7 record or render the reading\". Lines 92 and 273 do decide branches; the reading goes into `daysSince`, which then feeds the comparisons. That understates the risk, and it looks like a detector bug worth fixing: the clock value flowing through a variable into a comparison isn't being tracked.\n- The strongest date findings are all in `src/date.ts`: crime_00005 (mixed UTC and local methods, which really does produce wrong labels near midnight and New Year), crime_00009 (date strings parsed without a timezone), and crime_00014 (adding 86400000 ms as \"one day\"). They're real bugs, but they don't explain this failure. Nothing imports `date.ts` (blast radius 0), and `billing.ts` doesn't use it.\n- crime_00014's DST concern does apply to `billing.ts:92/273`, but the scan never flags it there because the code writes `1000*60*60*24` instead of the literal `86400000`.\n- I asked to scan `src/billing.ts`, but the report covers the whole repo (39 findings in 21 files), so the path argument seems to be ignored.\n- `src/billing.test.ts` doesn't exercise `billing.ts` at all: both tests call local stubs, one has no assertions (crime_00025) and the other only checks that the result is truthy (crime_00030). If real billing tests are flaking, they're somewhere else, or these stubs are covering for the missing coverage.\n\n## Proposed fix\n\nPass the clock in, count whole calendar days in UTC, and keep the logic in one helper instead of two copies:\n\n```ts\nconst MS_PER_DAY = 86_400_000;\n\n/** Whole calendar days between two instants, counted in UTC (no DST, no host TZ). */\nexport function daysBetween(fromMs: number, toMs: number): number {\n  const a = new Date(fromMs), b = new Date(toMs);\n  const startA = Date.UTC(a.getUTCFullYear(), a.getUTCMonth(), a.getUTCDate());\n  const startB = Date.UTC(b.getUTCFullYear(), b.getUTCMonth(), b.getUTCDate());\n  return Math.round((startB - startA) / MS_PER_DAY); // exact: UTC days are always 24h\n}\n\nexport function generateInvoice(user: User, items: InvoiceLine[], paymentMethod?: string,\n                                now: () => number = Date.now): Invoice {\n  const nowMs = now();\n  const createdAt = nowMs;                              // was Date.now() at :57\n  const daysSince = daysBetween(user.createdAt, nowMs); // was :92\n  // ...use nowMs for runAt, :198, :238/:250 as well\n}\n\nexport function recommendUpgrade(user: User, now: () => number = Date.now): string {\n  const daysSince = daysBetween(user.createdAt, now()); // was :273\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\nTests then pin the clock and check the exact boundaries:\n\n```ts\nconst NOW = Date.UTC(2026, 10, 12, 0, 30);              // just after midnight: the worst case\nconst user = { ...base, plan: \"pro\", createdAt: Date.UTC(2025, 10, 11, 23, 59) };\nexpect(generateInvoice(user, items, \"card\", () => NOW).discountCents).toBe(/* 10% */);\n// plus cases at exactly 365/366, 180/181, 90/91, 7/8, 14/15, 30/31 days, and a span crossing a DST change\n```\n\nTwo decisions are up to you:\n- **Is UTC the right calendar?** If \"a year as a customer\" should follow the customer's local time, pass a timezone and use `Intl` or Temporal instead.\n- **Should exactly 365 days count?** The `>` thresholds deliberately exclude day 365. Now's a good time to decide whether that was intended.\n\nAs a stopgap without changing any code, `vi.useFakeTimers(); vi.setSystemTime(...)` in the tests, plus `TZ=UTC` in the test script, makes the tests stable. That only hides the problem, though: production still has the DST and time-of-day drift.\n\nThe `date.ts` findings are worth fixing separately but aren't urgent for this bug. Line 41 in particular combines a UTC year with a local, zero-indexed month and has no zero-padding.\n\nI haven't changed any files or run any tests. The fixture's tests don't call `billing.ts`, so I couldn't reproduce the failure.",
